<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Awlad al-nas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as awlād al-nās</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Awlad al-nas, &quot;sons of respectable people&quot;, was the name for the sons of mamluks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1250 to 1517. Born free and Muslim, they were excluded from the closed military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> their fathers had joined. Only men who had grown up outside Muslim lands and entered service as slaves, to be converted, trained in barracks and freed, could join it. The awlad al-nas served instead in scribal and administrative posts or as commanders of the halqa, troops who were not mamluks.</p></div>
